-- title:
--   Toric points of coprime orders: ab lies in a join b
-- statement:
--   Fix natural numbers $p$ and $M$ with $p$ prime and $M \neq 0$, a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^{\times}$, and a divisibility $p \mid M$. Let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lying over $p$ in the sense that $p$ belongs to the non-units of $A$, and assume the residue field of $A$ has characteristic $p$ and is algebraically closed. Let $\Lambda$ be level data for $(p, M, H, A)$, that is, a structure morphism $\mathrm{Spec}\,A \to$ the base of level $p$ compatible with the generic point, together with a scheme $X$ over that base carrying a relative group law and bijections identifying the points of $J_H$ at level $M/p$ with the sections over the generic point and the degree-zero divisor class group of the reduced curve over the residue field of $A$ with the sections over the residual point. Let $O$ be an associated Néron object: a scheme $G$ over the base of level $p$, smooth, separated, locally of finite type, quasi-compact and surjective with preconnected fibres, equipped with a commutative relative group law, a bijection $\mathrm{pts}$ from $J_H(M,H)$ onto the sections over the generic point which is additive and Galois-equivariant, Hecke endomorphisms indexed by the generators $T_\ell$, $U_q$ and $\langle d\rangle$, and the remaining data of the structure. For positive coprime $a$ and $b$, the conclusion is the inequality of additive subgroups of $J_H(M,H)$ $$O.\mathrm{toricPts}(ab) \le O.\mathrm{toricPts}(a) \sqcup O.\mathrm{toricPts}(b),$$ where for $m > 0$ the subgroup $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(m)$ is generated by the range of the toric points of order dividing $m$ attached to $O$, and the join on the right is the subgroup generated by the two.
--
--   This records the elementary multiplicativity of the toric part of the Néron object of $J_H(M)$ at a prime $p$ exactly dividing the level: points of composite order decompose along a coprime factorisation. It is used in the analysis of torsion in the toric part, reducing prime-to-$p$ orders to prime powers, and is cited by [`ModularCurve.JHNeronObjectAtP.exists_forall_mem_toricPts_exists_smul_sub_eq_of_coprime_of_abelJacobiPin_of_atkinLehner`](thm.html#ModularCurve.JHNeronObjectAtP.exists_forall_mem_toricPts_exists_smul_sub_eq_of_coprime_of_abelJacobiPin_of_atkinLehner).
